# linux-rt.fragment — kernel-config delta for the RT / 7.2 beta variant.
#
# Layered on board/mister/de10nano/linux.config by Buildroot at kernel-build
# time (BR2_LINUX_KERNEL_CONFIG_FRAGMENT_FILES, set in configs/mister_rt.fragment).
# See docs/rt-beta-kernel.md.
#
# The base linux.config reconciles cleanly to 7.2 via `olddefconfig` (verified:
# every MiSTer-critical symbol survives), so this fragment carries ONLY the
# deltas that make this the RT/beta kernel: PREEMPT_RT itself, the UIO
# doorbell support that the RT line exists to serve, and pstore/ramoops so a
# board that dies on this kernel can say what it was doing.

# --- PREEMPT_RT: the point of the variant ------------------------------------
# A fully-preemptible real-time kernel. On Linux 7.2, arch/arm selects
# ARCH_SUPPORTS_RT in-tree (arch/arm/Kconfig:45), and EXPERT=y is inherited from
# linux.config, so PREEMPT_RT is directly selectable — there is NO out-of-tree
# RT patch to carry. (RT for 32-bit ARM merged into mainline in Linux 7.1.)
CONFIG_PREEMPT_RT=y

# --- UIO doorbells: FPGA-to-HPS f2h_irq8..15 into userspace ------------------
# ADR-002-interim (Main_MiSTer rewrite plan): stop polling the FPGA cause
# register over the lightweight bridge; block on read() of /dev/uioN instead.
# The device-tree half is board/mister/de10nano/linux-patches-beta/
# 0043-dts-uio-doorbells.patch — eight "generic-uio" nodes on GIC SPI 48..55
# (f2h_irq8..15; renumbered off SPI 40/41, which carry stock HDMI_TX_VS and
# video_sync per sys_top.v — see 0044/0045 for the region + writecombine half)
# (SPI 40 is vsync and stays with the MiSTer_fb driver).
#
# RT-ONLY, and that asymmetry is the whole point. linux-patches/ has no series
# file, so the stock 6.18 build applies every patch in it, this one included —
# but CONFIG_UIO is not set in the shared board/mister/de10nano/linux.config,
# so on stock those nodes match no driver, create no device and cost nothing
# but DTB bytes. These two symbols are what turn them into real /dev/uioN
# devices, and they live here so the stock kernel config stays byte-untouched.
CONFIG_UIO=y
CONFIG_UIO_PDRV_GENIRQ=y

# --- The cmdline parameter uio_pdrv_genirq needs when it is built in ---------
# uio_pdrv_genirq ships with an EMPTY of_match_table; the compatible string it
# matches is written into that table at runtime by its of_id parameter
# (module_param_string(of_id, uio_of_genirq_match[0].compatible, 128, 0) —
# drivers/uio/uio_pdrv_genirq.c). As a module that would be a modprobe
# argument. Built in (=y above, so the doorbells exist before userspace does),
# the kernel command line is the only way to set it — without the line below
# the DTS nodes bind to nothing and no /dev/uio* appears.
#
# This fragment is also the RT line's ONLY bootargs surface. There is no
# per-line U-Boot environment in this repo: U-Boot is stock and unmodified for
# both kernels (uboot-patches/ is empty; docs/rt-beta-kernel.md §5), and
# /media/fat/linux/u-boot.txt selects only WHICH zImage loads — it carries no
# command-line content. The real cmdline arrives via U-Boot's stock
# `setenv bootargs` plus its /chosen FDT fixup (docs/boot-chain.md §4.2).
#
# EXTEND, never FORCE: under CMDLINE_EXTEND, early_init_dt_scan_chosen()
# appends CONFIG_CMDLINE *after* whatever /chosen supplied (drivers/of/fdt.c),
# so U-Boot's root=, console= and the rest survive and we contribute exactly
# one token. CMDLINE_FORCE would overwrite them and the board would not boot.
# The two symbols must be set together: arch/arm/Kconfig gates the whole
# "Kernel command line type" choice on `depends on CMDLINE != ""`, and the
# shared linux.config leaves CONFIG_CMDLINE empty, so no choice is selected
# there at all — hence no conflict with the stock line.
CONFIG_CMDLINE="uio_pdrv_genirq.of_id=generic-uio"
CONFIG_CMDLINE_EXTEND=y

# --- pstore/ramoops: a crash record that outlives the crash -------------------
# The device-tree half is board/mister/de10nano/linux-patches-beta/
# 0046-dts-ramoops.patch — a /reserved-memory node at 0x1FE00000+0x100000 (the
# last MiB below the mem=511M cap), split 23×32 KiB dmesg records / 256 KiB
# console / 32 KiB pmsg. Its header carries the address and split arithmetic.
#
# Motivation, in one line: hardware finding H-1 hung the board three times with
# a serial console attached and produced ZERO bytes of output each time. The
# UART is not a crash channel — it stops being serviced at the instant of the
# hang. DRAM is, and it is still readable on the next boot.
#
# Each symbol below is load-bearing; none is decoration.
#
#   PSTORE          the subsystem itself (fs/pstore/Kconfig: tristate,
#                   default n, so it must be asked for). =y and not =m: the
#                   console frontend has to be registered before the failures
#                   worth recording, which are as likely during boot as after
#                   it, and a module loaded from the rootfs is far too late.
#   PSTORE_RAM      the ramoops backend — the only one that can work here.
#                   pstore/blk would write crash data to the SD card through
#                   the block layer, which a wedged CPU cannot drive; ramoops
#                   needs nothing but DRAM that keeps its contents. `depends on
#                   PSTORE` and pulls REED_SOLOMON* in by `select`, so those
#                   are not listed. =y for the same reason as above.
#   PSTORE_CONSOLE  the console frontend, and the one that actually addresses
#                   H-1. Without it the node's console-size is inert and
#                   pstore only records kmsg DUMPS — which a silent hang never
#                   triggers, because the kernel never reaches its die path.
#                   This is what captures printk output as it is produced,
#                   including the tail still sitting in the UART FIFO.
#   PSTORE_PMSG     /dev/pmsg0, the userspace breadcrumb channel. This is how a
#                   probe tool writes "about to touch LW +0x14" somewhere that
#                   survives the hang it is about to cause — the single fact
#                   all three H-1 investigations had to recover by bisection.
#                   `select RT_MUTEXES`, already y under PREEMPT_RT.
#
# NOT listed, deliberately: PSTORE_COMPRESS is `default y` and is inherited as
# such (it deflates dmesg records with the zlib library rather than the crypto
# API, specifically to avoid secondary faults while recording a panic);
# REED_SOLOMON* arrive via PSTORE_RAM's `select`; PSTORE_FTRACE stays unset
# even though it would resolve — FUNCTION_TRACER=y and DEBUG_FS=y are already
# in the shared linux.config, and PSTORE_FTRACE only `depends on` them, it
# selects nothing — because a fourth ramoops zone would shrink the three that
# serve H-1, for a per-function-call tracing job unrelated to it. The DT
# node's ftrace-size 0 keeps the two halves in lockstep.
#
# BETA-ONLY, and it has to be. The DTS node lives only in
# linux-patches-beta/, so the stock 6.18 DTB has no ramoops node at all;
# setting these symbols in the shared linux.config would give the stock kernel
# a pstore subsystem with nothing to bind to. The asymmetry is the same one
# CONFIG_UIO has above, in the same direction, for the same reason.
CONFIG_PSTORE=y
CONFIG_PSTORE_RAM=y
CONFIG_PSTORE_CONSOLE=y
CONFIG_PSTORE_PMSG=y

# NOTE: the RTL8814AU chip's in-kernel driver (CONFIG_RTW88_8814AU) is
# deliberately NOT set here — the main build migrated that chip to the in-kernel
# rtw88_8814au driver in the shared linux.config, so the beta inherits it
# automatically. Nothing to duplicate.

# NOTE: the HPS watchdog needs NOTHING here, and that was checked rather than
# assumed — see docs/rt-beta-kernel.md §9. The `snps,dw-wdt` nodes are upstream
# in socfpga.dtsi, socfpga_cyclone5.dtsi already does `&watchdog0 { status =
# "okay"; }`, and the shared linux.config already carries CONFIG_WATCHDOG=y and
# CONFIG_DW_WATCHDOG=y. /dev/watchdog0 therefore exists on BOTH kernels today.
# There is no patch to write and no symbol to add: a watchdog whose chardev is
# never opened is a driver that has bound and done nothing, which is exactly
# the safe default wanted. Do not "enable" it here — enabling is a userspace
# decision (open the device and feed it), and adding symbols would only create
# the appearance of a beta-only capability that is not beta-only.
